  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 400 AM CDT Tue Sep 29 2026\n\nUpper level wave with a plume of moisture will continue to push\neastward this morning. Meanwhile at the surface, a low pressure\nsystem and an associated stationary front will also slowly push\neastward this morning. The result will be lingering showers and\nthunderstorms this morning, mainly east of Highway 83. With\nlimited shear and instability, the main concern with these\nthunderstorms will be small hail and torrential rainfall. High\nPwat values of an 1 to 1.5 inches will remain with this\ndisturbance. Slow storm motion will also help with heavy rain\nthis morning. Thus localized flooding is possible, especially to\nareas that have received abundant rain in the last 24 hours.\nWill maintain the current Flood Watch as thunderstorms move\nthrough. Clearing skies behind this front has brought areas of\ndense fog to northwestern and north central portions. Issued a\nDense Fog Advisory as a result to areas already reporting fog.\nThis may need to be expanded depending how quickly skies clear\nelsewhere. Otherwise, drying conditions will be found once rain\nmoves out today. An increased zonal flow gradient aloft could\nbring some breezy westerly winds today. This combined with some\nlower RH values in the 20s could bring a brief period of\nelevated fire weather concerns this afternoon. Right now\nconditions do not appear to quick line up for critical fire\nweather concerns. Temperatures today could remain above normal\nwith this warm and dry westerly flow, with highs in the 70s.\nTonight, a weak cold front moves through while a dry southwest\nflow remains. Look for cooler lows in the mid 40s to mid 50s,\nyet mainly dry conditions. The steady wind and overall dry\nconditions should limit the fog threat tonight. This weak front\ncontinues to push through on Wednesday, perhaps continuing\nbreezy northwest winds. Temperatures look to cool slightly with\n60s in the north and 70s in the south. Mainly dry conditions are\nalso expected for Wednesday. Surface ridge then enters the area\nWednesday night into Thursday morning. This could bring cooler\nlow temperatures in the mid 30s to mid 40s, and possibly some\npatchy frost.\n\nRest of the work week could then be influenced by this surface \nhigh bringing cooler temperatures and generally light winds. \nMainly dry conditions are also expected, although perhaps a weak\nboundary brings a few showers in the southwest on Thursday. \nPatchy frost will be possible each morning with lows in the mid \n30s to mid 40s. Highs may also be slightly below normal and in \nthe 60s. Surface high moves eastward this weekend which could \nopen the door for passing upper level disturbances. Moisture \nlooks to be more limited for this weekend, thus PoPs are \ngenerally low. Temperatures also look to remain near to slightly\nbelow normal, which could provide patchy frost each morning.",
